## Why This Is Asked

This question tests understanding of the Command pattern, undo/redo implementation, and state management in a minimal system.

## Key Concepts

- Command pattern with execute/undo methods
- Receiver (TextEditor) and Command objects
- History stack for undo/redo operations
- State capture for reversible operations
- Edge cases: boundary checking, no-op commands

## Code Example

```javascript
class Editor {
  constructor() { this.text = ''; }

  insert(pos, ch) {
    this.text = this.text.slice(0, pos) + ch + this.text.slice(pos);
  }

  delete(pos, len) {
    const removed = this.text.slice(pos, pos + len);
    this.text = this.text.slice(0, pos) + this.text.slice(pos + len);
    return removed;
  }
}

class InsertCommand {
  constructor(editor, pos, ch) {
    this.editor = editor;
    this.pos = pos;
    this.ch = ch;
  }

  execute() {
    this.editor.insert(this.pos, this.ch);
  }

  undo() {
    this.editor.delete(this.pos, 1);
  }
}

class History {
  constructor() {
    this.undoStack = [];
    this.redoStack = [];
  }

  execute(command) {
    command.execute();
    this.undoStack.push(command);
    this.redoStack = [];
  }

  undo() {
    if (this.undoStack.length === 0) return;
    const command = this.undoStack.pop();
    command.undo();
    this.redoStack.push(command);
  }
}

// Example: Insert 'A' at position 0, then undo
const editor = new Editor();
const history = new History();
const insertA = new InsertCommand(editor, 0, 'A');

history.execute(insertA); // text = 'A'
history.undo(); // text = ''
```

## Implementation Notes

- Commands capture all state needed for undo
- History maintains separate undo/redo stacks
- Each operation is atomic and reversible
- Position validation ensures safe operations

## Why This Is Asked
The question probes ability to design a dynamic, runtime-extensible cross-cutting concerns system using proxies, with per-method interceptor ordering and async support. It tests API design, runtime extensibility, and trade-offs among common approaches.

## Key Concepts
- Proxy-based interception for runtime extensibility
- Interceptor registry and per-method order
- Async support via promises and proper error propagation
- Trade-offs: Proxy vs Decorator vs Interceptor-chain

## Code Example
```javascript
type Context = { target: any; method: string; args: any[] };
interface Interceptor {
  name: string;
  intercept(ctx: Context, next: () => Promise<any>): Promise<any>;
}
class Registry {
  private map = new Map<string, () => Interceptor>();
  register(name: string, factory: () => Interceptor) { this.map.set(name, factory); }
  createChain(names: string[]): Interceptor[] { return names.map(n => this.map.get(n)!).filter(Boolean); }
}
function createProxy<T>(target: T, registry: Registry, perMethod: Map<string, string[]>): T {
  return new Proxy(target as any, {
    get(obj, prop: string) {
      const orig = (obj as any)[prop];
      if (typeof orig !== 'function') return orig;
      return (...args: any[]) => {
        const order = perMethod.get(prop) || [];
        const chain = registry.createChain(order);
        let idx = -1;
        const next = (): Promise<any> => {
          idx++;
          if (idx >= chain.length) return Promise.resolve(orig.apply(obj, args));
          const interceptor = chain[idx];
          return interceptor.intercept({ target: obj, method: prop, args }, next);
        };
        return next();
      };
    }
  });
}
```

